<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Toronto Poster Columns &mdash; Reference Layer for OSM Editors</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="index.css">
<link rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
      integrity="sha256-p4NxAoJBhIIN+hmNHrzRCf9tD/miZyoHS5obTRR9BMY="
      crossorigin="">
<script defer src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"
        integrity="sha256-20nQCchB9co0qIjJZRGuk2/Z9VM+kNiyxNV1lvTlZBo="
        crossorigin=""></script>
<script defer src="index.js"></script>
<script data-goatcounter="https://osm-toronto.goatcounter.com/count" async src="//gc.zgo.at/count.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<h1>Toronto Poster Columns</h1>
<p class="lead">
  A reference layer of Toronto's community postering spots, the places you can
  pin a flyer, as the City publishes them: <strong>{{COLUMN_COUNT}}</strong>
  poster columns and <strong>{{BOARD_COUNT}}</strong> poster boards on transit
  shelters. Ready to add to the <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a>
  editors <strong>iD</strong> and <strong>JOSM</strong> while you map.
</p>
<p class="meta">City data: {{DATA_DATE}} &middot; tiles rebuilt: {{BUILD_DATE}}</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <a href="{{LICENSE_URL}}">{{LICENSE_NOTICE}}</a>
  Sources: <a href="{{COLUMN_DATASET}}">Street Furniture &ndash; Poster Structure</a>
  and <a href="{{BOARD_DATASET}}">Street Furniture &ndash; Poster Board</a>.
</div>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Where you may post.</strong> Toronto's sign by-law
  (<a href="https://www.toronto.ca/legdocs/municode/1184_693.pdf">Municipal
  Code Chapter 693</a>, Article 5, &sect;&nbsp;693-5.2 and 5.3) keeps posters
  off City property except on structures the City has designated for them,
  and sets the size, fixing and time limits there. The
  <strong>poster columns</strong> are those structures. Whether the public may
  post on the <strong>shelter boards</strong> is <strong>unconfirmed</strong>;
  they are in this layer because the City publishes them as poster boards, not
  because anyone has confirmed they are open to all.
</div>

<h2>Preview</h2>
<div id="preview-map" data-raster-url="{{RASTER_URL}}"></div>
<p class="meta">
  The raster layer drawn over OpenStreetMap, from zoom 15:
  <span class="key column"></span> poster column (circle),
  <span class="key shelter_board"></span> shelter poster board (square). A
  hollow marker is one the City lists as <em>Temporarily Removed</em>.
</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <strong>Coverage vs OpenStreetMap:</strong> see which City postering spots
  are <a href="gaps/">missing from OSM, misplaced or mapped twice</a> &mdash; a
  mapper's worklist with an interactive map and table.
</div>

<h2>Two layers &mdash; which to use</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Vector layer</th>
    <td>Interactive. Click a point to read the City's fields
        (<code>kind</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>site_id</code>,
        <code>asset_type</code>, <code>city_status</code>,
        <code>address</code>, <code>fronting</code>,
        <code>from_street</code>). Best in <strong>iD</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Raster layer</th>
    <td>Plain markers, circles and squares in two colours, no labels. A readable backdrop. Best in
        <strong>JOSM</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Do not copy the City's numbers into <code>ref</code>.</strong>
  <code>id</code> (<code>PO-&hellip;</code>, <code>SH-&hellip;</code>) and
  <code>site_id</code> are the City's asset numbers; a mapper standing at the
  column cannot check either. The dot tells you where to look. OSM already
  maps these both ways &mdash; as <code>advertising=*</code> (mostly
  <code>board</code>, some <code>column</code> or <code>poster_box</code>) or
  as a notice board (<code>tourism=information</code> +
  <code>information=board</code> + <code>board_type=notice</code>) &mdash; and
  the gap page counts either.
</div>

<h2>Add to iD</h2>
<h3>Vector layer (interactive)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the iD editor and press <kbd>U</kbd> for the <em>Map Data</em> panel.</li>
  <li>Under <em>Custom Map Data</em>, open the settings and paste this URL:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL}}</pre>
<p>Zoom in, then click any point to see its fields in the inspector.</p>

<h3>Raster layer</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the <em>Background</em> panel and choose <em>Custom</em>.</li>
  <li>Paste this URL template:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{RASTER_URL}}</pre>

<h2>Add to JOSM</h2>
<h3>Raster layer (recommended for JOSM)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> and click <strong>+TMS</strong>.</li>
  <li>Paste this entry (the <code>tms[15,17]</code> prefix sets the zoom range;
      JOSM scales it up past z17):</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">tms[15,17]:{{RASTER_URL_JOSM}}</pre>
<p>
  Give it a name such as <em>Toronto Poster Columns</em>, click OK, then
  enable it from the <em>Imagery</em> menu. Note JOSM's placeholder is
  <code>{zoom}</code>, not <code>{z}</code>.
</p>

<h3>Vector layer (optional)</h3>
<p>
  JOSM can also add the vector tiles via <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> &rarr;
  <strong>+MVT</strong>:
</p>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL_JOSM}}</pre>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The City's lists are its own street furniture. A notice board outside a
      library, a church or a community centre is not in them, so an OSM board
      with no dot under it is not wrong &mdash; the gap page lists those as
      &ldquo;OSM only&rdquo; for a look, not a fix.</li>
  <li>A shelter board sits on a transit shelter. OSM usually has the shelter
      (most of the City's boards are within 10&nbsp;m of one) but almost never
      the board on it.</li>
  <li>Street-level imagery is the way to check a spot from home; aerial
      imagery will not show a poster column.</li>
  <li>Vector tiles are pre-rendered for z12&ndash;19; raster tiles for
      z15&ndash;17 (the editor overzooms them further).</li>
  <li>Tiles are rebuilt weekly; the City refreshes both datasets about twice
      a year.</li>
  <li>This is a <strong>reference</strong> layer, not an import &mdash; add a
      column to OpenStreetMap when you have seen it.</li>
</ul>

